1
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
An immortal legend as you've only imagined.

2
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Tarzan is a family, adventure, animation film directed by Chris Buck.

3
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Tarzan was a small orphan who was raised by an ape
named Kala since he was a child.

4
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:46,000
He believed that this was his family, but on an
expedition Jane Porter is rescued by Tarzan.

5
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:26,000
He then finds out that he's human.

6
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,000
Now Tarzan must make the decision as to which family he should belong to...

7
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:46,000
Tarzan (voice), played by Tony Goldwyn.

8
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:26,000
Jane Porter (voice), played by Minnie Driver.

9
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Kala (voice), played by Glenn Close.

10
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:46,000
Young Tarzan (voice), played by Alex D. Linz.

11
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:26,000
Terk (voice), played by Rosie O'Donnell.

12
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,000
Clayton (voice), played by Brian Blessed.

13
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:46,000
Tarzan is a 1999 American animated adventure comedy-drama film based on
the 1912 story Tarzan of the Apes by Edgar Rice Burroughs.

14
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:26,000
Produced by Walt Disney Feature Animation, it was directed by Kevin Lima and Chris Buck, from a screenplay by Tab Murphy, and
the writing team of Bob Tzudiker and Noni White, and is the first major animated film adaptation of the story.

15
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Tony Goldwyn stars as the titular character, alongside Minnie
Driver, Glenn Close, Rosie O'Donnell, Alex D.

16
00:42:40,000 --> 00:42:46,000
Linz, Brian Blessed, Nigel Hawthorne, Lance Henriksen, and Wayne Knight.

17
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:26,000
The film follows an orphan who is raised by the family of gorillas in
Africa, after his real parents were killed by a leopard.

18
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:06,000
Years later, he grows up into a man and meets other humans.

19
00:50:40,000 --> 00:50:46,000
He is soon torn between the choice of leaving with
them or staying with his animal family.

20
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:26,000
Pre-production of Tarzan began in 1995, with Lima selected as
director and Buck joining him the same year.

21
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
Following Murphy's first draft, Tzudiker, White, Dave Reynolds, and Jeffrey Stepakoff (the latter two of whom received additional
screenplay credits in the final cut), were brought in to reconstruct the third act and add additional material to the screenplay.

22
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:46,000
English recording artist Phil Collins was recruited to compose and
record songs integrated with a score by Mark Mancina.

23
01:01:20,000 --> 01:01:26,000
Meanwhile, the production team embarked on a research trip
to Uganda and Kenya to study the gorillas.

24
01:04:00,000 --> 01:04:06,000
The animation of the film combines 2D hand-drawn animation with the extensive use of computer-generated imagery, and it was done in California, Orlando, and
Paris, with the pioneering computer animation software system Deep Canvas being predominantly used to create three-dimensional backgrounds.

25
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:46,000
Tarzan premiered at the El Capitan Theatre in Los Angeles on June 12,
1999, and was released in the United States on June 18.

26
01:09:20,000 --> 01:09:26,000
It received positive reviews from critics, who praised its voice
performances, music, animation, and action sequences.

27
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
Against a production budget of $130 million (then the most expensive traditionally animated film ever made until Treasure Planet in 2002), the film grossed $448.2 million worldwide, becoming the fifth highest-grossing
film of 1999, the second highest-grossing animated film of 1999 behind Toy Story 2, and the first Disney animated feature to open at first place at the North American box office since Pocahontas (1995).

28
01:14:40,000 --> 01:14:46,000
It won the Academy Award for Best Original Song
("You'll Be in My Heart" by Phil Collins).

29
01:17:20,000 --> 01:17:26,000
The film has led to many derived works, such as a Broadway adaptation, a television series,
and two direct-to-video followups, Tarzan & Jane (2002) and Tarzan II (2005).

30
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
Due to licensing issues with Edgar Rice Burroughs, Inc.,
the use of these characters is limited.

31
01:22:40,000 --> 01:22:46,000
The film explores themes of baby, africa, gorilla, adoption, cartoon.

32
01:25:20,000 --> 01:25:26,000
The tone is emotional.
